<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter Fraser</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter Fraser was a Scottish-born <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> Labour politician who was prime minister of New Zealand from 1940 to 1949. He led the country through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, built on the welfare state founded under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-joseph-savage">Michael Joseph Savage</a>, and helped draft the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-charter">United Nations Charter</a> in 1945, especially its trusteeship provisions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-fraser.jpg" alt="Peter Fraser" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Office of War Information · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Fraser.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fraser was born at Hill of Fearn, near Tain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-ross">Easter Ross</a>, and left school early because his family was poor. Apprenticed to a carpenter, he gave up the trade because of very poor eyesight, and later in life he asked for spoken rather than written reports. He read socialists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keir-hardie">Keir Hardie</a> and Robert Blatchford, became secretary of the local Liberal Association at 16 and joined the Independent Labour Party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1908. Unable to find work there, he emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> in 1910.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> he worked on the wharves, joined the New Zealand Socialist Party and managed the campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-joseph-savage">Michael Joseph Savage</a>. After an arrest for breaches of the peace in 1913 he turned from direct action to the parliamentary road. He helped found the Labour Party in 1916 and later that year was jailed for a year for sedition after opposing conscription, a verdict he always rejected. Elected for Wellington Central in 1918, he came to reject the Bolshevik leaders and pressed for communists to be kept out of the party. In 1923 he lost the Wellington mayoralty by only 273 votes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In Savage's cabinet from 1935 he held external affairs, health, education and other portfolios, often working seventeen hours a day, and named C. E. Beeby to the Education Department. As prime minister he introduced conscription, which he had opposed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and some called his style authoritarian. In 1943 he brought 6,000 battle-weary soldiers home on furlough, and many refused to return. He signed the Australian-New Zealand Agreement of 1944 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/h-v-evatt">H. V. Evatt</a>.</p><p>Wartime reforms ended flogging in 1941, gave all workers two weeks of annual leave in 1944, raised the school leaving age to 15 and set a minimum wage in 1945. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> conference of 1945 he opposed the veto of the permanent members of the Security Council and often spoke for smaller states. His government adopted the Statute of Westminster in 1947, but slow removal of rationing and peacetime compulsory military training cost it support. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> on 12 December 1950 and is buried in Karori Cemetery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peter Fraser” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>New Zealand became a fully independent state in law when Parliament passed the Statute of Westminster Adoption Act in November 1947, under the Labour government of Prime Minister <b>Peter Fraser</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Peter Fraser</b> led the country through the Second World War.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-new-zealand">Dominion of New Zealand</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tain, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=57.81,-4.06&amp;year=1926" aria-label="Tain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Peter Fraser"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4815 5181h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4815 5181h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=57.81,-4.06&amp;year=1926">What was here in 1926? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
